--TEST--
`Core\Response::setHeader` refuses the one header a body member owns, in every case its name can be spelled -- spec § 15 and `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, not four separate answers: a field name is case-insensitive, so a
// member that refused only the spelling it was written against would leave the
// mislabelling § 4 exists to prevent one lower-case letter away — `json` for
// the body, `text/html` for the label, and the browser believing the label.
array<string> $spellings = ["Content-Type", "content-type", "CONTENT-TYPE", "cOnTeNt-TyPe"];
uint $refused = 0;
foreach ($spellings as string $name) {
    try {
        Core\Response::setHeader($name, "text/html");
    } catch (LogicError $owned) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
    }
}
echo "refused ", $refused, " of 4 spellings\n";

// Its neighbours are not it. A name that merely begins or ends the same way is
// an ordinary header, which is what makes the refusal above a rule about one
// header rather than about a prefix.
array<string> $neighbours = ["Content-Length", "Content-Encoding", "X-Content-Type", "Content-Type-Options"];
uint $set = 0;
foreach ($neighbours as string $near) {
    try {
        Core\Response::setHeader($near, "0");
        $set = $set + 1;
    } catch (LogicError $rejected) {
    }
}
echo "set ", $set, " of 4 neighbours\n";

// And the sentence says where that header went rather than only that it is
// refused: a program naming a media type has a member that takes one, beside
// the body it describes.
try {
    Core\Response::setHeader("content-type", "text/html");
} catch (LogicError $why) {
    echo $why->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
refused 4 of 4 spellings
set 4 of 4 neighbours
Core\Response::setHeader(): `content-type` is declared by the body member that wrote the body — answer with `Core\Response::bytes($body, $contentType)` to say what a body is
